    "note_en": "An article is a small word placed before a noun, a naming word. Italian il libro means “the book”; la carta means “the leaf” or “the sheet”. Il marks masculine singular here and la feminine singular. Grammatical gender belongs to the Italian noun: it is not a statement that a book is male or a leaf female. Learn il libro, la carta, la tavola (the table), la porta (the door), and la cassa (the chest) together. This is a deliberately small article subset, not a rule for every Italian noun. Latin has no directly equivalent article. The singular forms developed historically from demonstrative forms including illum and illam; those Latin references explain the history, not a claim that Italian il always means “that”.\n\nÈ means “is”, with a written grave accent. E without an accent means “and”: do not confuse the two. Il libro non è sulla tavola means “The book is not on the table”. Non comes before the verb: non è means “is not”. In the story, Il libro è sulla tavola means “The book is on the table”. In this chapter learn sulla tavola (“on the table”), nella cassa (“in the chest”), and nel libro (“in the book”) as three supported location phrases. Their internal preposition/article combinations are not a productive target yet.\n\nThe actions are shown only in the forms used by one named person: cerca “searches for”, guarda “looks at”, apre “opens”, trova “finds”, porta “carries”, legge “reads”, chiude “closes”. A third-person form describes someone spoken about, such as Luca or Marta. Luca cerca il libro means “Luca searches for the book”. Porta also occurs as the noun “door”: la porta is “the door”, while Luca porta il libro is “Luca carries the book”. Context distinguishes them. Do not infer a full conjugation from these forms. Marta porta la carta sulla tavola means “Marta carries the leaf onto the table”. La carta è sciolta means “The leaf is loose”: it is unattached, rather than one of the bound leaves. Finding it inside a book does not establish its owner or whether it belongs to the volume. Those questions remain open.\n\nThe dictionary forms (infinitives), such as cercare “to search for”, are reference labels, not an extra conjugation target. Copia means “copies”: Marta cannot start copying while the book is missing. She begins after it returns, then pauses to examine the unexpected leaf. Only this one form of copiare is used here.",
    "bridge_notes": [
      {
        "lang": "la",
        "text": "illum",
        "target_text": "il"
      },
      {
        "lang": "la",
        "text": "illam",
        "target_text": "la"
      }
    ]
  },
